ou start talking about mike a guy  that's  wow  227 228 pounds can run you over in the  hall moves the pile forward every time i  watch him and i mean when you know  there's two yards right mike and the  hole is filled with two linebackers he  gets four yards he always falls forward  and keeps things moving that way let  alone again this is why i say these two  guys are first-round talents he's got  some elite traits yes the physical the  pat you know the physicality and the  power i talked about but this is the  fastest guy of the group i mean it  doesn't matter really what game you turn  on he can turn the corner and run for 70  yard touchdowns or split the middle of  the ohio state defense and run for an 80  you know a 50 yard touchdown he has game  breaking type speed and that's special  you just don't see a whole lot of guys  coming around the nfl very often who  were 228 pound running backs and as  thick and powerful as he is then also be  able to run 439 so yes i look at those  two guys as clearly my top two running  backs and two guys that i do think have  the talent uh to to justify a first  round pick  taylor has a very electric engaging  personality i was able to him at the  scouting combine before the world turned  upside down but he's got a lot of wear  and tear he's got a lot of carries a lot  of yards and you know some people look  at a guy's career as the full college  pro experience combined and there's only  so many touches there's only so many  yards before the tread comes off the  tires and taylor has put in a lot of  work chris at the college level are you  concerned about that well yeah i am to a  degree of course i mean you have to take  that into account now he's built like a  brick house you know so that's one thing  like you saw him in person you could  he's one of those guys where like you go  man if i stuck a knife into his arm i'm  not sure it would go in the knife might  bend you know so he was built and met  meant to play this position in the nfl  you know and there's been also listen  there's been other plenty of running  backs who have been bell cows hey mark  ingram's still going derrick henry still  going so i'm not worried from that  standpoint but here's the big point mike  to me  we got to stop thinking of like oh we're  drafting a running back and he's going  to be our guy for the next eight years  no that's just not the way it is  drafting a running back you gotta look  at it like hey i got a four five maybe  five year window  things are probably gonna break down or  fall off at the end of this rookie  contract and if they don't it's just  icing on the cake and oh great man we  got lucky and got a guy that we can give  a second contract to but i think really  when you're doing the running back  position this day and age right now you  almost have to be expecting i'm really  only gonna have this guy four or five  years and i'm probably gonna have to  move on or draft somebody else once  again too  yeah but actually in this day and age  the reality is the player has a great  four or five years so great that you  have to sign him to the second contract  and then when you do  it starts to fall apart that's the  concern and the players are pushing for  the deals earlier like ezekiel elliott  last year like christian mccaffrey will  this year like dalvin cook may in  minnesota but  it feels like you get the best out of  these guys year one year two year three  and then it starts to plateau or dip and  the problem is when you have that  workhorse tailback that's why i think  it's great to have two or three guys  when you have one guy who becomes that  superstar who is generating a ton of  yardage a bunch of touchdowns for the  fantasy football crowd and everybody  buys his jersey and he's everyone's  favorite player  then you have to pay him it makes it so  much harder to rip that band-aid off and  let him leave look at what the steelers  went through with le'veon bell and they  were lucky that bell wasn't more  identified as the star of the team  because they had antonio brown they had  ben roethlisberger and that's a team  that relies so heavily on defense but  for so many teams that running back  becomes bigger than the franchise  what do you do  you pay him and then you regret it chris  no i mean you're right and i think  that's one reason one more reason to  have you know a little bit of a running  back to my committee you can make a guy  that's maybe your focal point running  back superstar guy like you're talking  about maybe last a little longer to  where he can get into that second  contract and still have some value there  but you know and the big thing to me  mike is yeah i mean listen this is just  a harsh reality you're right and it's a  tough situation for teams and certainly  they're gonna have to be careful i wish  they could find a way  to pay these guys  better  in their first four to five years of  their career where it's like the running  back only counted like 80 of his  contract against the salary cap or  something like that because to what  you're saying it's one horror story  after another you know it's aryan  foster leading the nfl twice and rushing  before he gets paid  i can think of alfred morris with the  redskins and the washington redskins  with kyle shanahan you know on his  rookie contract he was a top five rusher  in the nfl twice but never gets the big  payday because like you're saying by the  time it's year five he's beat to hell  and he's got nothing left and that's  where i really have a soft place in my  heart for this position in the nfl  there was a push last year by a lawyer  in chicago and she was trying to create  a separate bargaining unit for running  backs and there is right merit to that  we went through that discussion last  month with the cba you've got a bunch of  different interests and a bunch of  different levels of contact values of  contract length of career and the  running back has huge value early in his  career and then it diminishes and unless  you are that first-round pick you're not  getting rewarded for what you do early  in your career and you just have to hope  that your team will take care of you  before the wheels start to come off  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports

